#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063964 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "4f78dc34-7cc7-5024-9641-b4ea74e4d155" :question/math-academy-id "e-3743" :question/problem "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) =  x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 4x^3 - 16 x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x),$ we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16,\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}-16=32>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-16=-16<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-16=32>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2,$ so these are relative minima."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "088bc8a4-e82a-586b-89b5-49482f579c4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3744" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x.$ Given that $f'(5) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f''(5)  >           0$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=  x^3-6x^2-15x,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) =  3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0.$ Indeed, we can see that $f'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0.$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5) & =6(5)-12 \\\\  & =30-12 \\\\  & =18>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c17c5262-a191-5f8f-809b-35a8463a2485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303574} {:answer/id #uuid "f8085f68-bcb5-5048-a333-8119c7f54e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303575} {:answer/id #uuid "772ca79f-acab-57f8-aac1-9b7ce244ec4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303576} {:answer/id #uuid "121e1b32-aa44-58a6-aeaa-2df101a75838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303577} {:answer/id #uuid "83525aaf-af2f-5586-b3d4-c0529c291449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83525aaf-af2f-5586-b3d4-c0529c291449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303578} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0216f1f-c5ba-5956-b2b3-3db49c2e1d2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d08953a7-b9c1-53e4-92d6-e017849bbea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-21508" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
